QUESTIONS.push(
/* ---------------- Naming variants: numeric/alphabetic and SNP nomenclature (pp. 22–24) ---------------- */
{id:"L18-001", lecture:"L18", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L18", sub:"nomen", skill:"recall", multi:true, core:true, coreWhy:"her four naming systems; she said every detail of these examples is to be remembered",
 concept:"four-nomenclature-systems", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Which naming systems for genetic variants appear in the pharmacogenomics literature? Select all that apply.",
 options:[
  {t:"Allele numeric/alphabetic nomenclature", correct:true, why:"The first of the four: gene symbol, nucleotide position and the nucleotide, as in VKORC1 1173 C."},
  {t:"Single nucleotide polymorphism (SNP) nomenclature", correct:true, why:"The second: the wild type nucleotide, an arrow (>), then the variant nucleotide, as in ABCB1 3435 C>T."},
  {t:"Star nomenclature", correct:true, why:"The third: gene symbol, a star and a number, as in CYP2C19*2; all cytochrome P450s are named this way."},
  {t:"Reference SNP (rs) nomenclature", correct:true, why:"The fourth: a unique rs number from the SNP database, as in rs4986893."},
  {t:"Hardy-Weinberg nomenclature", correct:false, why:"Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium is a frequency term from 10/20, not a way of naming a variant."}],
 teach:"There are four ways genetic variants are named in the literature: allele numeric/alphabetic, single nucleotide polymorphism (SNP) nomenclature, star nomenclature and reference SNP (rs) nomenclature. Which system a gene gets depends on how the scientists who first described the variant chose to name it. The same real genes (VKORC1, ABCB1, CYP2C19) recur later as clinical examples.",
 quote:"we have 4 different ways that you'll see in the literature that genetic variants are named.",
 cite:"Intro to PGx Lecture Students Fall 2026.pdf slides ‘Polymorphism Nomenclature’ (p. 22) to ‘Reference SNP (rs) Nomenclature’ (p. 27); (T) 10/21 transcript"},

{id:"L18-002", lecture:"L18", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L18", sub:"nomen", skill:"apply",
 concept:"numeric-alphabetic-parts", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"In the variant name VKORC1 1173 C, what does 1173 indicate?",
 options:[
  {t:"The nucleotide's location on the gene", correct:true, why:"Counting nucleotides down from the start codon, 1173 is the 1,173rd nucleotide of the VKORC1 gene."},
  {t:"The amino acid that is changed", correct:false, why:"The name gives a nucleotide position and a nucleotide (C or T), not an amino acid."},
  {t:"The variant's number in the SNP database", correct:false, why:"A database number is the rs form, written rs followed by digits (rs4986893)."},
  {t:"The order in which the variant was found", correct:false, why:"Order of discovery is what a star number reflects (CYP2C19*3 was found after *2)."}],
 teach:"Allele numeric/alphabetic nomenclature combines letters and numbers: the first letters and numbers identify the gene (VKORC1), the number gives the location of the nucleotide on the gene (1173), and the letter is the nucleotide found there (C or T). People have been found with either a C or a T at the 1,173rd nucleotide of VKORC1.",
 quote:"the numbers here are indicating where in the gene, the genetic variant is occurring",
 note:"The transcript renders VKORC1 as \"VKRC1\", \"VQRC1\" and \"VQORC1\".",
 cite:"Intro to PGx Lecture Students Fall 2026.pdf slide ‘Allele Numeric/Alphabetic Nomenclature’ (p. 23); (T) 10/21 transcript"},

{id:"L18-003", lecture:"L18", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L18", sub:"nomen", skill:"tell",
 concept:"numeric-alphabetic-drawback", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"What is the main weakness of allele numeric/alphabetic names such as VKORC1 1173 C?",
 options:[
  {t:"They name the gene with an unofficial gene symbol", correct:false, why:"The symbol (VKORC1) is the standard one; her complaint is about which nucleotide is the wild type, not the gene name."},
  {t:"Which allele is wild type is not always clear", correct:true, why:"Writers are supposed to capitalize the wild type nucleotide and leave the variant lowercase, and she is not certain they do."},
  {t:"They give no location on the gene", correct:false, why:"The number (1173) is the location."},
  {t:"Each name can refer to more than one SNP", correct:false, why:"Uniqueness is not her complaint here; the rs system's strength is that no other SNP shares a number."}],
 teach:"In the numeric/alphabetic system the convention is to capitalize the wild type nucleotide and write the variant in lowercase, but that is not always done. A paper that mentions only VKORC1 1173C also does not tell a reader who does not know the gene what the other version is. SNP nomenclature fixes this by writing wild type > variant.",
 quote:"it's not always super clear which version is the wild type and which version is the variant. People are supposed to be capitalizing the wild type, nucleotide, OK, and leaving in lowercase the variant form",
 cite:"Intro to PGx Lecture Students Fall 2026.pdf slide ‘Allele Numeric/Alphabetic Nomenclature’ (p. 23); (T) 10/21 transcript"},

{id:"L18-004", lecture:"L18", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L18", sub:"nomen", skill:"apply", core:true, coreWhy:"in-class question with her answer",
 concept:"snp-nomenclature-wildtype", tags:["smith","inclass"], source:"both",
 stem:"In the single nucleotide polymorphism (SNP) name ABCB1 3435 C>T, which nucleotide is the wild type?",
 options:[
  {t:"T", correct:false, why:"T, after the arrow, is the variant identified in people."},
  {t:"C", correct:true, why:"In SNP nomenclature the wild type nucleotide is always written first, then the arrow, then the variant."},
  {t:"Neither; a database lookup is needed", correct:false, why:"That is the rs system; SNP nomenclature shows the wild type directly."},
  {t:"Whichever letter is capitalized", correct:false, why:"Capitalization is the convention of the numeric/alphabetic system, not the SNP system."}],
 teach:"SNP nomenclature names the gene, the nucleotide position, the original (wild type) nucleotide first, an arrow (>), and then the nucleotide it changes to. For ABCB1 3435 C>T, C is the wild type and T is the variant. The arrow is what makes it clear which is which.",
 quote:"We're going to put the nucleotide, that's the wild type, first, then you do the little arrow carrot. And then you put, what is the variant that has been identified",
 note:"Her question to the room: \"which is the wild type\"; the class answered C, and she added that the variant identified in people is T.",
 cite:"Intro to PGx Lecture Students Fall 2026.pdf slide ‘SNP Nomenclature’ (p. 24); (T) 10/21 transcript"},

{id:"L18-005", lecture:"L18", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L18", sub:"nomen", skill:"recall",
 concept:"abcb1-pgp", tags:["smith","inclass"], source:"both",
 stem:"The gene ABCB1 codes for which protein?",
 options:[
  {t:"Thiopurine methyltransferase", correct:false, why:"That enzyme is coded by TPMT."},
  {t:"Cytochrome P450 2D6", correct:false, why:"That enzyme is coded by CYP2D6."},
  {t:"P-glycoprotein", correct:true, why:"ABCB1 is one of the ABC family of transporter genes; its product is P-glycoprotein."},
  {t:"Cytochrome P450 2C19", correct:false, why:"That enzyme is coded by CYP2C19."}],
 teach:"ABCB1 belongs to the ABC family of transporter genes and codes for the P-glycoprotein transporter. Here the gene name (ABCB1) and the protein name (P-glycoprotein) differ, so it is easy to tell which is meant.",
 quote:"So that's the name of the gene, codes for P-glycoprotein",
 note:"She asked the room what ABCB1 codes for; a student said a transporter, and then P-glycoprotein.",
 cite:"Intro to PGx Lecture Students Fall 2026.pdf slide ‘SNP Nomenclature’ (p. 24); (T) 10/21 transcript"},

{id:"L18-006", lecture:"L18", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L18", sub:"nomen", skill:"recall",
 concept:"gene-italics", tags:["smith"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"How is a gene symbol such as ABCB1 written in pharmacogenomics literature, compared with its protein?",
 options:[
  {t:"Gene in capitals, protein in lowercase", correct:false, why:"Case is not the distinction she gave; TPMT is written in capitals as both gene and protein."},
  {t:"Gene in italics, protein not in italics", correct:true, why:"Italics separate the gene from the protein, which matters when they share a name, as TPMT does."},
  {t:"Protein in italics, gene not in italics", correct:false, why:"Reversed."},
  {t:"Both in italics", correct:false, why:"Then the two could not be told apart when the names are the same."}],
 teach:"A gene symbol is italicized and the protein is not. Sometimes the code for a gene is the same as the name of its protein: TPMT in italics is the gene, and TPMT not italicized is the enzyme thiopurine methyltransferase. For ABCB1 the protein has a different name, P-glycoprotein.",
 quote:"when we write the name of a gene or the code for a gene like ABCB 1 here, it should be italicized.",
 cite:"Intro to PGx Lecture Students Fall 2026.pdf slides ‘Synonymous SNP: P-Glycoprotein’ (p. 34), ‘Non-Synonymous SNP: TPMT’ (p. 35); (T) 10/21 transcript"},

/* ---------------- Star nomenclature (pp. 25–26) ---------------- */
{id:"L18-007", lecture:"L18", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L18", sub:"star", skill:"recall", core:true, coreWhy:"star nomenclature convention",
 concept:"star1-wildtype", tags:["smith","inclass"], source:"both",
 stem:"In star nomenclature, which allele is the wild type for any gene?",
 options:[
  {t:"*1", correct:true, why:"By convention *1 is always the wild type version of whatever gene is named."},
  {t:"*0", correct:false, why:"No *0 allele appears in her examples; numbering starts at *1."},
  {t:"The allele with the highest star number", correct:false, why:"Higher numbers are variants, numbered as they were found."},
  {t:"Whichever allele is most common in that population", correct:false, why:"Frequency differs by population; the *1 label does not change."}],
 teach:"In star nomenclature the first letters and numbers identify the gene (CYP2C19), and the star with a number designates the allele. By convention *1 is always the wild type, and *2, *3 and higher are variants. At the poll she asked the room for the wild type symbol, and the answer was star one.",
 quote:"By convention. We always call star one for whatever gene we're talking about, that's the wild type version",
 cite:"Intro to PGx Lecture Students Fall 2026.pdf slide ‘Allele “Star” Nomenclature’ (p. 25); (T) 10/21 transcript"},

{id:"L18-008", lecture:"L18", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L18", sub:"star", skill:"recall",
 concept:"star-cyp450", tags:["smith"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"Which group of genes is always named with star nomenclature?",
 options:[
  {t:"The ABC transporter genes", correct:false, why:"ABCB1 appears in her SNP nomenclature example (3435 C>T)."},
  {t:"The vitamin K-related genes", correct:false, why:"VKORC1 appears in the numeric/alphabetic and SNP examples and has haplotypes A and B."},
  {t:"The cytochrome P450 genes", correct:true, why:"All cytochrome P450s are named with star nomenclature, a convention started by the scientist who identified some of the first CYP variants."},
  {t:"The thiopurine methyltransferase gene only", correct:false, why:"TPMT uses star names (TPMT*3A), but it is not the only gene that does."}],
 teach:"Star nomenclature is used for several kinds of genes, but all cytochrome P450 genes are named this way. The convention came from Sid Nelson at the University of Washington, who identified some of the first cytochrome P450 variants and wrote the original tables that define each star allele.",
 quote:"This is used for various types of genes, but all of our cytochrome P450s are named using the star nomenclature.",
 cite:"Intro to PGx Lecture Students Fall 2026.pdf slide ‘Allele “Star” Nomenclature’ (p. 25); (T) 10/21 transcript"},

{id:"L18-009", lecture:"L18", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L18", sub:"star", skill:"recall", core:true, coreWhy:"the defining change of CYP2C19*2 and *3; used again on the rs and stop codon slides",
 concept:"cyp2c19-star-definitions", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Which nucleotide changes define CYP2C19*2 (cytochrome P450 2C19) and CYP2C19*3?",
 options:[
  {t:"*2: 636 G>A; *3: 681 G>A", correct:false, why:"Reversed: *2 is at 681 and *3 at 636."},
  {t:"*2: 681 C>T; *3: 636 C>T", correct:false, why:"Both changes are G to A."},
  {t:"*2: 681 G>A; *3: 636 G>A", correct:true, why:"The slide's parentheses give the change behind each star name: *2 at nucleotide 681 and *3 at nucleotide 636, both G to A."},
  {t:"*2: 1173 C>T; *3: 3435 C>T", correct:false, why:"Those positions belong to VKORC1 and ABCB1."}],
 teach:"CYP2C19*2 is a G to A change at nucleotide 681, and CYP2C19*3 is a G to A change at nucleotide 636. Papers write only CYP2C19*2 or *3; the change behind each star is found in the tables that define the alleles. She put the changes in parentheses on the slide to show what the stars mean.",
 quote:"what SAR 2 means for SIP2C19, it means we're looking at the nucleotide 681 and the wild type is G. And the variant that we found is A.",
 note:"\"SAR 2\" and \"SIP2C19\" are the caption's renderings of *2 and CYP2C19. Later in the session she said of *3: \"I think it's at the 481. I'm sorry, it's at 263. I'd have to go back and look at my slide\"; the slide (p. 25) and her earlier statement (\"change at nucleotide 636\") give 636, which is keyed.",
 cite:"Intro to PGx Lecture Students Fall 2026.pdf slide ‘Allele “Star” Nomenclature’ (p. 25); (T) 10/21 transcript"},

{id:"L18-010", lecture:"L18", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L18", sub:"star", skill:"apply",
 concept:"star-lookup-tables", tags:["smith"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"A paper reports patients who are CYP2C19*2 (cytochrome P450 2C19) carriers and gives no other detail. How does a reader find what change *2 is?",
 options:[
  {t:"Check the tables that define star alleles", correct:true, why:"Papers do not put the change in parentheses; the defining tables (the originals written by Sid Nelson) give the location and change."},
  {t:"Read it from the star number itself", correct:false, why:"The number carries no position or base information; *3 is even upstream of *2."},
  {t:"Assume it matches CYP2C9*2", correct:false, why:"The same star number in another gene is a different locus."},
  {t:"Count 2 nucleotides down from the gene's start codon", correct:false, why:"The star number is not a position."}],
 teach:"In the literature, star alleles appear without the underlying change: a paper says CYP2C19*2 or CYP2C19*3. Unless one has worked in the field for a long time, the star name alone does not say what the variant is. Tables, starting with Sid Nelson's originals, define each star allele by its location and nucleotide change.",
 quote:"You have to dive down and dig into it a bit to know what is star 2, OK? So there are tools out there, you can find tables on the internet.",
 cite:"Intro to PGx Lecture Students Fall 2026.pdf slide ‘Allele “Star” Nomenclature’ (p. 25); (T) 10/21 transcript"},

{id:"L18-011", lecture:"L18", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L18", sub:"star", skill:"apply",
 concept:"star-number-discovery-order", tags:["smith","inclass"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"CYP2C19*3 (cytochrome P450 2C19) lies closer to the start codon than CYP2C19*2. Why?",
 options:[
  {t:"Star numbers count backward from the stop codon", correct:false, why:"Star numbers are not positions at all."},
  {t:"*3 is a deletion that shifted the numbering", correct:false, why:"*3 is a single G to A change."},
  {t:"*3 is the more common allele", correct:false, why:"Frequency does not set the number."},
  {t:"*3 was identified after *2", correct:true, why:"Star numbers follow the order in which variants were characterized, so their locations can be scrambled along the gene."}],
 teach:"She drew the gene with the start codon on the left and asked whether *3 sits to the right or left of *2. *3, at 636, is toward the start codon, and the class gave the reason: it was found later. Star numbers follow the order of discovery, so in a table the variants appear in numerical order while their locations on the gene are scrambled.",
 quote:"it's because they identified the star 2 variant first. And later they characterized the star 3 variant, so it was the next one.",
 note:"Her questions: \"Is 3 to the right or the left?\" and \"why did they put 3? To the left\"; the accepted answer: \"Yes, it was found later.\"",
 cite:"Intro to PGx Lecture Students Fall 2026.pdf slide ‘Allele “Star” Nomenclature’ (p. 25); (T) 10/21 transcript"},

{id:"L18-012", lecture:"L18", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L18", sub:"star", skill:"tell", core:true, coreWhy:"her 'don't assume' slide; she checked it with a turn-to-your-neighbor",
 concept:"same-star-different-gene", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"What is the functional effect of the *2 allele in cytochrome P450 2C19 (CYP2C19) compared with CYP2C9?",
 options:[
  {t:"CYP2C19*2: decreased activity; CYP2C9*2: no activity", correct:false, why:"Reversed."},
  {t:"CYP2C19*2: no activity; CYP2C9*2: decreased activity", correct:true, why:"CYP2C19*2 makes a truncated, non-functional protein; CYP2C9*2 lowers the enzyme's activity without removing it."},
  {t:"Both: no activity", correct:false, why:"CYP2C9*2 keeps some activity."},
  {t:"Both: decreased activity", correct:false, why:"CYP2C19*2 has none."}],
 teach:"For CYP2C19, *1 is normal (wild type) activity and *2 and *3 have no enzyme activity. For CYP2C9, *1 is normal and *2 and *3 have decreased activity. The labels look exactly the same, but the functional effect depends on the specific protein.",
 quote:"2C192 has no enzyme activity. 2C92 has decreased enzyme activity.",
 note:"\"2C192\" and \"2C92\" are the caption's renderings of CYP2C19*2 and CYP2C9*2.",
 cite:"Intro to PGx Lecture Students Fall 2026.pdf slide ‘More Allele “Star” Nomenclature’ (p. 26); (T) 10/21 transcript"},

{id:"L18-013", lecture:"L18", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L18", sub:"star", skill:"recall", multi:true, core:true, coreWhy:"her two take-home points, checked in class",
 concept:"dont-assume-two-points", tags:["smith","inclass"], source:"both",
 stem:"CYP2C19 and CYP2C9 both have *1, *2 and *3 alleles. Which of these must be looked up for each gene rather than assumed from the star number? Select all that apply.",
 options:[
  {t:"Where *2 is located on the gene", correct:true, why:"CYP2C9*2 is not at nucleotide 681 the way CYP2C19*2 is; each lab numbers its own gene's variants as found."},
  {t:"What functional effect *2 has", correct:true, why:"CYP2C19*2 has no activity; CYP2C9*2 has decreased activity."},
  {t:"Whether *1 is the wild type allele", correct:false, why:"No lookup is needed: *1 is the wild type for every gene."},
  {t:"Where *3 is located on the gene", correct:true, why:"CYP2C9*3 is not at 636 the way CYP2C19*3 is."}],
 teach:"Two things cannot be assumed across genes: that a star number marks the same spot on the gene, and that it has the same functional significance. CYP2C19*2 (681) and CYP2C9*2 are at different loci, and CYP2C19*2 has no activity while CYP2C9*2 has decreased activity. The one thing shared across genes is that *1 is the wild type.",
 quote:"just because 2 is at one place in one gene doesn't mean it's in the same place in another gene, right? And just because 2 in one gene means that gene's product is inactive, doesn't mean star 2 is going to be inactive in another gene's product",
 note:"She asked the class to turn to a buddy and explain \"the two points I just made\": \"They deal with location and functional significance of the gene.\"",
 cite:"Intro to PGx Lecture Students Fall 2026.pdf slide ‘More Allele “Star” Nomenclature’ (p. 26); (T) 10/21 transcript"},

{id:"L18-014", lecture:"L18", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L18", sub:"star", skill:"recall",
 concept:"cyp2c19-star2-truncated", tags:["smith"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"Why does the CYP2C19*2 (cytochrome P450 2C19) allele produce no active enzyme?",
 options:[
  {t:"The whole CYP2C19 gene is deleted from the allele", correct:false, why:"Whole gene deletion is CYP2D6*5."},
  {t:"It codes for a short, truncated protein", correct:true, why:"The *2 allele results in a really short protein that is too short to be functional; *3 also gives a truncated protein."},
  {t:"It is a tandem repeat in the gene", correct:false, why:"Tandem repeats are a different variant type; *2 is a single G to A change at 681."},
  {t:"It adds extra copies of the gene", correct:false, why:"Extra copies raise activity (CYP2D6*2xN)."}],
 teach:"CYP2C19*2 results in a really short, non-functional protein, so a person with that allele makes no active enzyme from it. By chance, CYP2C19*3 also gives a truncated protein that is too short to be functional. CYP2C9*2 and *3, by contrast, change the enzyme so its activity decreases but is not obliterated.",
 quote:"The star 2 Allele. Actually results in a really short nonfunctional protein.",
 cite:"Intro to PGx Lecture Students Fall 2026.pdf slide ‘More Allele “Star” Nomenclature’ (p. 26); (T) 10/21 transcript"},

{id:"L18-015", lecture:"L18", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L18", sub:"star", skill:"apply", core:true, coreWhy:"her own example exam question, with her answer",
 concept:"cyp2c19-33-no-activity", tags:["smith","inclass"], source:"both",
 stem:"True or false: a person who is homozygous for the *3 variant of cytochrome P450 2C19 (CYP2C19*3/*3) has CYP2C19 enzyme activity.",
 options:[
  {t:"True", correct:false, why:"Both copies are *3, and *3 makes no active enzyme."},
  {t:"False", correct:true, why:"*3/*3 means the copy from each parent is *3; neither copy makes active enzyme, so there is no CYP2C19 activity."}],
 teach:"We are diploid, with two copies of each gene, one from each parent. A person whose copy from the mother and copy from the father are both *3 is homozygous *3/*3. *3 produces a truncated, inactive protein, so this person has no CYP2C19 activity.",
 quote:"homozygous for the 3 variant of 2C19 has 2C19 enzyme activity, true or false? False.",
 note:"She offered this as an example of how she could ask it on an exam, prefaced: \"I don't usually use true or false, but how about this?\"",
 cite:"Intro to PGx Lecture Students Fall 2026.pdf slides ‘More Allele “Star” Nomenclature’ (p. 26), ‘Genotype Nomenclature’ (p. 28); (T) 10/21 transcript"},

{id:"L18-016", lecture:"L18", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L18", sub:"star", skill:"recall",
 concept:"genotype-phenotype-tight", tags:["smith"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"For CYP2C19*2 (cytochrome P450 2C19) and the CYP2C9 variants, how strong is the evidence linking genotype to enzyme activity?",
 options:[
  {t:"Weak; activity has only been inferred from drug responses", correct:false, why:"The activity of each variant has been measured directly in the lab."},
  {t:"Tight; the variants are well characterized in the lab", correct:true, why:"Variant enzymes have been made, expressed and their activity measured against the wild type, so the association is strong."},
  {t:"Unknown; these variants have not been tested", correct:false, why:"These are among the best characterized variants."},
  {t:"Strong only for CYP2C9", correct:false, why:"She described both genes as well characterized."}],
 teach:"The variant (*2) is the genotype and the enzyme's activity is the phenotype: a genotype-phenotype relationship. For the CYP2C19 and CYP2C9 variants on this slide, labs have made the variant genes, expressed the enzymes and measured their activity against the wild type, so the strength of the association is tight.",
 quote:"the strength of the association between the genotype and phenotype is tight, we've got great data on these.",
 cite:"Intro to PGx Lecture Students Fall 2026.pdf slide ‘More Allele “Star” Nomenclature’ (p. 26); (T) 10/21 transcript"},

{id:"L18-017", lecture:"L18", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L18", sub:"star", skill:"term",
 concept:"functional-significance-1021", dupOf:"L17-039", tags:["smith"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"What does the functional significance of a genetic variant describe?",
 options:[
  {t:"How common the variant is in a population", correct:false, why:"That is allele frequency."},
  {t:"Where on the gene the variant is found", correct:false, why:"That is its location (locus), which a star number does not give."},
  {t:"What the variant does to the protein's activity", correct:true, why:"The protein can be an enzyme, a transporter or a receptor; the question is what happens to its activity."},
  {t:"Which drugs are metabolized by the gene's protein product", correct:false, why:"Affected drugs follow from the gene, not from the variant's effect."}],
 teach:"Functional significance is what a variant in a gene does to the activity of the protein the gene codes for, whether an enzyme, a transporter or a receptor. For CYP2C19*2 in humans, the enzyme does not work: no activity. She repeated this point twice and called it a really key concept.",
 quote:"What happens to the function of the gene product because of the variant?",
 cite:"Intro to PGx Lecture Students Fall 2026.pdf slide ‘More Allele “Star” Nomenclature’ (p. 26); (T) 10/21 transcript"},

/* ---------------- Reference SNP (rs) nomenclature (p. 27) ---------------- */
{id:"L18-018", lecture:"L18", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L18", sub:"rsnum", skill:"apply", core:true, coreWhy:"the three names of one variant",
 concept:"rs4986893-cyp2c19-3", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"The variant rs4986893 is the same variant as which of these?",
 options:[
  {t:"CYP2C19*2 (681 G>A)", correct:false, why:"*2 is the 681 change; rs4986893 is the 636 change."},
  {t:"CYP2C9*3", correct:false, why:"A different gene."},
  {t:"ABCB1 3435 C>T", correct:false, why:"A different gene and a C to T change."},
  {t:"CYP2C19*3 (636 G>A)", correct:true, why:"One variant, three names: CYP2C19*3, CYP2C19 636 G>A and rs4986893."}],
 teach:"The same variant can carry three names: CYP2C19*3 (star), CYP2C19 636 G>A (SNP) and rs4986893 (reference SNP). Most papers say CYP2C19*3 because the other two take too long to say or remember. A reader needs to know what defines the star name and may have to search the database for an rs number.",
 quote:"this particular RS number is actually the RS name for SIP2C193, which you now already know is the 636g to A.",
 note:"\"SIP2C193\" is the caption's rendering of CYP2C19*3.",
 cite:"Intro to PGx Lecture Students Fall 2026.pdf slide ‘Reference SNP (rs) Nomenclature’ (p. 27); (T) 10/21 transcript"},

{id:"L18-019", lecture:"L18", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L18", sub:"rsnum", skill:"recall", multi:true,
 concept:"rs-facts", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Which statements about reference SNP (rs) nomenclature are correct? Select all that apply.",
 options:[
  {t:"No other SNP shares a given rs number", correct:true, why:"A new variant, once curated in the SNP database, gets its own number."},
  {t:"The Human Genome Variation Society recommends it as the standard", correct:true, why:"It is the official, recommended way to name SNPs."},
  {t:"The rs name shows the wild type nucleotide", correct:false, why:"It does not; the rs number gives no nucleotide at all."},
  {t:"The numbers come from the SNP database (dbSNP)", correct:true, why:"dbSNP is the single database for genetic variation information."},
  {t:"Most papers use the rs name for CYP2C19*3", correct:false, why:"Most papers say CYP2C19*3, though not always."}],
 teach:"In rs nomenclature a newly discovered variant is submitted to the SNP database (dbSNP), and once curated it receives a number no other SNP has, such as rs4986893. The Human Genome Variation Society recommends it as the standard way to name SNPs. It is useful because it points to exactly one SNP, but the numbers are cumbersome and the database is hard to query.",
 quote:"It's recommended by the Human Genome Variation Society to be the standard way that we name snips, OK? We do do that, but it's hard to use.",
 cite:"Intro to PGx Lecture Students Fall 2026.pdf slide ‘Reference SNP (rs) Nomenclature’ (p. 27); (T) 10/21 transcript"},

{id:"L18-020", lecture:"L18", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L18", sub:"rsnum", skill:"tell", core:true, coreWhy:"her Poll Everywhere question",
 concept:"poll-which-hides-wildtype", tags:["smith","poll"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"Which allele nomenclature method does not easily let you know which allele is the wild type?",
 options:[
  {t:"Star nomenclature", correct:false, why:"Star nomenclature has a symbol for the wild type: *1."},
  {t:"SNP nomenclature", correct:false, why:"The wild type is written first, before the arrow (C>T)."},
  {t:"Reference SNP (rs) nomenclature", correct:true, why:"An rs number marks a variant but does not say what the wild type is; it takes digging in the database."},
  {t:"Genotype nomenclature using star alleles", correct:false, why:"Genotypes are written with star alleles (*1/*2), so the *1 shows the wild type."}],
 teach:"She asked this on Poll Everywhere (not for a grade). Star nomenclature marks the wild type as *1, and SNP nomenclature puts the wild type before the arrow. The numeric/alphabetic system is supposed to capitalize the wild type, but she does not always trust that it is done. The rs number names a variant without saying what the wild type version is.",
 quote:"so the RS nomenclature doesn't clearly indicate to you what is the wild type",
 note:"Poll Everywhere, not graded; the poll's title would not display, so she read the question aloud. The answer options were not read into the recording; the options here are the systems she discussed. She also cast doubt on the numeric/alphabetic system (\"I'm not sure it always happens, so I don't always trust that\") but closed on rs as the answer.",
 cite:"Intro to PGx Lecture Students Fall 2026.pdf slides ‘Allele Numeric/Alphabetic Nomenclature’ (p. 23) to ‘Reference SNP (rs) Nomenclature’ (p. 27); Poll Everywhere (T) 10/21 transcript"},

/* ---------------- Genotype nomenclature and gene dose (p. 28) ---------------- */
{id:"L18-021", lecture:"L18", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L18", sub:"gtype", skill:"apply", multi:true, core:true, coreWhy:"genotype to activity table",
 concept:"cyp2c19-genotype-no-activity", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Which cytochrome P450 2C19 (CYP2C19) genotypes have no enzyme activity? Select all that apply.",
 options:[
  {t:"CYP2C19*2/*2", correct:true, why:"Homozygous for a no-activity allele."},
  {t:"CYP2C19*1/*3", correct:false, why:"The *1 allele makes active enzyme: reduced activity, not none."},
  {t:"CYP2C19*2/*3", correct:true, why:"Both alleles make no active enzyme."},
  {t:"CYP2C19*3/*3", correct:true, why:"Homozygous for a no-activity allele."},
  {t:"CYP2C19*1/*1", correct:false, why:"Two wild type alleles: normal activity."}],
 teach:"Genotype nomenclature writes both alleles a person carries. For CYP2C19: *1/*1 has normal (wild type) activity; *1/*2 or *1/*3 has reduced activity; *2/*2, *2/*3 and *3/*3 have no enzyme activity. Any combination of *2 and *3 has no activity because neither allele makes working enzyme.",
 quote:"Star 23 have no activity, so any combination of 2 and 3 together will have no activity.",
 cite:"Intro to PGx Lecture Students Fall 2026.pdf slide ‘Genotype Nomenclature’ (p. 28); (T) 10/21 transcript"},

{id:"L18-022", lecture:"L18", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L18", sub:"gtype", skill:"apply",
 concept:"cyp2c19-23-why-none", tags:["smith","inclass"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"Why does a person who is CYP2C19*2/*3 (cytochrome P450 2C19) have no CYP2C19 activity even though the two alleles differ?",
 options:[
  {t:"Heterozygotes always lose all activity", correct:false, why:"*1/*3 is also heterozygous and has reduced activity."},
  {t:"*3 suppresses the *2 allele", correct:false, why:"There is no dominance between these alleles."},
  {t:"Both *2 and *3 produce no active enzyme", correct:true, why:"Neither copy contributes activity, so the sum is zero."},
  {t:"The two variants form a haplotype on one chromosome", correct:false, why:"They are two different alleles, one on each chromosome, not variants traveling together on one."}],
 teach:"She asked who belongs in the no-activity group and why *2/*3 is in it. The answer: both the *2 and the *3 of CYP2C19 have no activity, so a heterozygote carrying one of each still has no CYP2C19 activity. Heterozygous does not mean partial activity unless one allele is active.",
 quote:"Why is 23 in this graph, and it has no activity? Cause both those variants, the 2 of 2C19 and the 3 of 2C19 have no activity",
 cite:"Intro to PGx Lecture Students Fall 2026.pdf slide ‘Genotype Nomenclature’ (p. 28); (T) 10/21 transcript"},

{id:"L18-023", lecture:"L18", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L18", sub:"gtype", skill:"term", core:true, coreWhy:"gene dose effect, named and explained by her",
 concept:"gene-dose-effect", tags:["smith","inclass"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"A CYP2C19*1/*3 (cytochrome P450 2C19) heterozygote has activity about halfway between *1/*1 and no activity. What is this called?",
 options:[
  {t:"Dominance", correct:false, why:"She said there is no dominance in the drug-response variants: the activities add up."},
  {t:"The gene dose effect", correct:true, why:"Activity reflects how many active alleles a person carries; one *1 gives middle activity."},
  {t:"Linkage disequilibrium", correct:false, why:"That is variants co-occurring more often than chance (haplotypes)."},
  {t:"Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium", correct:false, why:"That is about frequencies staying constant across generations."}],
 teach:"In a *1/*3 heterozygote, the protein from the *1 allele is active and the protein from the *3 allele is not, so added together the person has about half the CYP2C19 activity, which looks like decreased activity. She called this the gene dose effect. Unlike textbook dominant and recessive traits, these drug-response variants do not show dominance.",
 quote:"So in combination. If you were to add it up, they look halfway in the range of 2C19 activity.",
 note:"A student asked whether *1 is dominant and *2 and *3 recessive; she answered no, the activities add. She called this a simplified answer.",
 cite:"Intro to PGx Lecture Students Fall 2026.pdf slide ‘Genotype Nomenclature’ (p. 28); (T) 10/21 transcript"},

{id:"L18-024", lecture:"L18", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L18", sub:"gtype", skill:"tell",
 concept:"no-dominance", tags:["smith","inclass"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"Is the *1 allele of cytochrome P450 2C19 (CYP2C19) dominant over *2 and *3?",
 options:[
  {t:"Yes; a *1/*2 person has the same activity as a *1/*1 person", correct:false, why:"*1/*2 has reduced activity, not normal."},
  {t:"No; activity reflects the number of active alleles", correct:true, why:"There is no dominance; the active *1 allele contributes its activity and the inactive allele contributes none."},
  {t:"Yes; *1/*3 has no activity", correct:false, why:"That would make *3 the dominant one, and *1/*3 has reduced activity."},
  {t:"No; *2 overpowers *1", correct:false, why:"A student raised this; she answered \"No, not really. No.\""}],
 teach:"Dominant and recessive (the smooth and wrinkled peas) do not describe the drug-response variants. A *1/*2 or *1/*3 person keeps some activity from the *1 allele and lands in the middle. For now she keeps it simple: with a *1 allele there is always some activity.",
 quote:"In these gene variants that we're concerned about with drug response, there's not a dominance that happens",
 cite:"Intro to PGx Lecture Students Fall 2026.pdf slide ‘Genotype Nomenclature’ (p. 28); (T) 10/21 transcript"},

{id:"L18-025", lecture:"L18", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L18", sub:"gtype", skill:"apply",
 concept:"induction-cannot-rescue-star2", tags:["smith"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"A CYP2C19*1/*2 (cytochrome P450 2C19) patient takes something that induces CYP2C19. What happens to the activity that comes from the *2 allele?",
 options:[
  {t:"It rises to normal", correct:false, why:"Making more of a truncated protein still gives no working enzyme."},
  {t:"It stays at none", correct:true, why:"Induction can increase expression, but the *2 protein is too short to function."},
  {t:"It rises to half of normal", correct:false, why:"The *2 protein has no activity to scale up."},
  {t:"It becomes greater than the *1 allele's", correct:false, why:"The *2 protein has no activity to scale up."}],
 teach:"Diet or other drugs can induce an enzyme and raise its expression. In a *1/*2 person, expression may increase, but no activity comes off the *2 allele because that variant makes the protein too short to function. Only the *1 allele's contribution can increase.",
 quote:"you're still not going to get any activity off of star 2 because the way that gene variant happens, it's making the protein too short and not functional.",
 cite:"Intro to PGx Lecture Students Fall 2026.pdf slide ‘Genotype Nomenclature’ (p. 28); (T) 10/21 transcript"},

{id:"L18-026", lecture:"L18", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L18", sub:"gtype", skill:"apply", lowYield:true,
 concept:"cyp2c9-induction-aside", tags:["smith"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"Why could induction blur the activity groups for cytochrome P450 2C9 (CYP2C9) more than for CYP2C19?",
 options:[
  {t:"CYP2C9 has no *1 allele", correct:false, why:"CYP2C9*1 is the wild type."},
  {t:"CYP2C9*2 and *3 keep some activity", correct:true, why:"An enzyme with a little activity can be induced; one with none cannot."},
  {t:"CYP2C9 cannot be induced", correct:false, why:"She described something that induces CYP2C9."},
  {t:"CYP2C9 variants are whole gene deletions", correct:false, why:"CYP2C9*2 and *3 are changes that decrease activity."}],
 teach:"CYP2C19*2 and *3 are inactive, but CYP2C9*2 and *3 are a little active. If someone ingests something that induces CYP2C9, the *2 or *3 enzyme could be induced enough to change the clean normal, middle and none pattern. She said there is little data on this and she would never ask it.",
 quote:"It is possible, but I will never ask you a question on this.",
 note:"Low yield: she said she will never ask this. For the exam she keeps it clean: CYP2C19 *1 is active, *2 and *3 are not, heterozygotes are middle of the road.",
 cite:"Intro to PGx Lecture Students Fall 2026.pdf slide ‘Genotype Nomenclature’ (p. 28); (T) 10/21 transcript"},

/* ---------------- Haplotype nomenclature (p. 29) ---------------- */
{id:"L18-027", lecture:"L18", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L18", sub:"haplonom", skill:"recall", core:true, coreWhy:"VKORC1 haplotypes, to return with warfarin",
 concept:"vkorc1-haplotypes", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"VKORC1 has at least 10 SNPs that may have a functional effect. How are its most common combinations of these SNPs named?",
 options:[
  {t:"Haplotype A and haplotype B", correct:true, why:"Different groupings of the SNPs make up haplotype A and haplotype B."},
  {t:"VKORC1*1 and VKORC1*2", correct:false, why:"Some genes give haplotypes star names, but VKORC1's are lettered."},
  {t:"rs1173 and rs3435", correct:false, why:"Those are positions from other examples written as rs numbers; not haplotype names."},
  {t:"Fast haplotypes and slow haplotypes", correct:false, why:"Those are phenotype words, not the haplotype names."}],
 teach:"A haplotype is a set of variants at several loci on one allele that occur together more often than random chance. VKORC1 has at least 10 SNPs with functional significance, many of which occur in haplotypes; the most common are named haplotype A and haplotype B. The scientists who discover a haplotype name it, so other genes use other names, sometimes star names.",
 quote:"There's one called haplotype A, and there's another called haplotype B.",
 cite:"Intro to PGx Lecture Students Fall 2026.pdf slide ‘Haplotype Nomenclature’ (p. 29); (T) 10/21 transcript"},

{id:"L18-028", lecture:"L18", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L18", sub:"haplonom", skill:"recall",
 concept:"why-name-haplotypes", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Why are haplotypes such as VKORC1 haplotype A given a single name?",
 options:[
  {t:"Naming is required before publication", correct:false, why:"She gave no such rule."},
  {t:"It is simpler than listing each SNP", correct:true, why:"Saying \"haplotype A\" replaces naming several changes in a row."},
  {t:"Each haplotype is a single SNP", correct:false, why:"A haplotype is several variants together."},
  {t:"The name gives each SNP's position", correct:false, why:"The letter carries no position information."}],
 teach:"The slide asks how to describe the multiple SNPs that may co-exist in one person at the same time. Her answer: name the haplotype, because saying each change in turn is too much. VKORC1 haplotypes A and B have functional significance and return in the context of warfarin.",
 quote:"We just say it's haplotype A, and this person's haplotype B.",
 note:"She tried to list the changes in a VKORC1 haplotype from memory (\"281, G to C, and ... 263 or 463 C to T\"); these are not on the slide and are not tested.",
 cite:"Intro to PGx Lecture Students Fall 2026.pdf slide ‘Haplotype Nomenclature’ (p. 29); (T) 10/21 transcript"},

{id:"L18-029", lecture:"L18", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L18", sub:"haplonom", skill:"apply",
 concept:"vkorc1-warfarin", tags:["smith"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"The VKORC1 variants and haplotypes will return as clinical examples in the dosing of which drug?",
 options:[
  {t:"Codeine", correct:false, why:"Codeine is affected by CYP2D6 variants."},
  {t:"Azathioprine", correct:false, why:"Azathioprine is affected by TPMT variants."},
  {t:"Omeprazole", correct:false, why:"Omeprazole is affected by CYP2C19 variants."},
  {t:"Warfarin", correct:true, why:"VKORC1 is the gene she will discuss with warfarin dosing."}],
 teach:"She uses real genes as nomenclature examples because they come back as clinical examples. The VKORC1 variant (1173) and haplotypes A and B are important for warfarin dosing. The frequency figure on 10/21 also showed VKORC1 -1639G>A alleles labeled by dose need (G, higher dose required; A, lower dose required).",
 quote:"this VQORC1 genetic variant is important, and we'll talk about it in the context of warfarin dosing",
 note:"\"VQORC1\" is the caption's rendering of VKORC1.",
 cite:"Intro to PGx Lecture Students Fall 2026.pdf slides ‘Allele Numeric/Alphabetic Nomenclature’ (p. 23), ‘Haplotype Nomenclature’ (p. 29); (T) 10/21 transcript"},

/* ---------------- SNPs and the coding types (pp. 30–34) ---------------- */
{id:"L18-030", lecture:"L18", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L18", sub:"snp", skill:"term",
 concept:"snp-definition-1021", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Which description fits a single nucleotide polymorphism (SNP)?",
 options:[
  {t:"A single base pair in a gene is changed", correct:true, why:"One base substitution; several million have been identified across the genome."},
  {t:"A short sequence is inserted or deleted", correct:false, why:"That is an InDel."},
  {t:"A short sequence repeats one after another", correct:false, why:"That is a tandem repeat."},
  {t:"A whole gene is copied or lost", correct:false, why:"That is a copy number variant or gene deletion."}],
 teach:"A SNP is a single base pair change in a gene. Millions have been identified across the human genome, and a SNP may or may not alter the function of the gene's product. SNPs that change nothing have still been identified.",
 quote:"a single base pair has been changed in the gene. Um, millions have been identified across the human genome.",
 cite:"Intro to PGx Lecture Students Fall 2026.pdf slides ‘SNPs’ (p. 32), ‘SNP’ (p. 33); (T) 10/21 transcript"},

{id:"L18-031", lecture:"L18", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L18", sub:"snp", skill:"recall", multi:true, core:true, coreWhy:"the coding SNP types she uses for every later example",
 concept:"coding-snp-types", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Which are coding single nucleotide polymorphism (SNP) types? Select all that apply.",
 options:[
  {t:"Synonymous", correct:true, why:"The base changes but the amino acid does not (ABCB1 3435C>T)."},
  {t:"Non-synonymous", correct:true, why:"The base change changes the amino acid (TPMT*3A)."},
  {t:"Premature stop codon", correct:true, why:"The base change creates a stop codon early (CYP2C19*3)."},
  {t:"Microsatellite", correct:false, why:"A microsatellite is a tandem repeat of a short sequence, not a single base change."}],
 teach:"SNPs are classed as coding or non-coding. Coding regions are the parts of a gene that are translated to RNA and then to protein. Within coding regions, SNPs are synonymous, non-synonymous or premature stop codons, and these are the ones of interest in pharmacogenomics.",
 quote:"within the coding regions of genes, we'll classify snips as being synonymous or non-synonymous. Or being premature stop codons.",
 cite:"Intro to PGx Lecture Students Fall 2026.pdf slide ‘SNPs’ (p. 32); (T) 10/21 transcript"},

{id:"L18-032", lecture:"L18", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L18", sub:"snp", skill:"apply", core:true, coreWhy:"in-class question with her answer",
 concept:"abcb1-3435-synonymous", tags:["smith","inclass"], source:"both",
 stem:"In ABCB1 3435C>T the wild type codon ATC becomes ATT. Has the amino acid sequence of P-glycoprotein changed?",
 options:[
  {t:"Yes; it is a non-synonymous SNP", correct:false, why:"Non-synonymous requires a different amino acid."},
  {t:"No; it is a synonymous SNP", correct:true, why:"ATC and ATT both bring in isoleucine, so the protein sequence is unchanged."},
  {t:"Yes; it creates a premature stop codon", correct:false, why:"ATT is not a stop codon; the stop codon example is TAG in CYP2C19*3."},
  {t:"No; it is a non-coding SNP", correct:false, why:"3435 is in the coding sequence; the codon is translated."}],
 teach:"At position 3435 of ABCB1 the wild type C gives the codon ATC, which brings in isoleucine. With the C to T change the codon is ATT, which still codes for isoleucine. Synonymous means the same: the DNA changed, but the protein's amino acid sequence did not.",
 quote:"Synonymous means the same. The change resulted in still the same protein, no change to the protein",
 note:"She asked twice, \"have we had a change in the amino acid sequence\"; she saw some heads nodding yes and gave the answer: no.",
 cite:"Intro to PGx Lecture Students Fall 2026.pdf slide ‘Synonymous SNP: P-Glycoprotein (P-gp)’ (p. 34); (T) 10/21 transcript"},

{id:"L18-033", lecture:"L18", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L18", sub:"snp", skill:"apply",
 concept:"why-study-synonymous", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Why do studies of drug response keep examining ABCB1 3435C>T?",
 options:[
  {t:"It keeps appearing in studies of drug response", correct:true, why:"It turns up repeatedly with different P-gp substrates; she thinks it probably affects how much protein is expressed."},
  {t:"It truncates P-glycoprotein", correct:false, why:"Truncation is CYP2C19*2 and *3; this SNP leaves the protein intact."},
  {t:"It deletes the ABCB1 gene", correct:false, why:"Whole gene deletion is CYP2D6*5."},
  {t:"It changes the isoleucine at that codon to threonine", correct:false, why:"Isoleucine is unchanged; alanine to threonine is TPMT*3A."}],
 teach:"A synonymous SNP is worth studying when it keeps popping up in studies of patients' drug response. For ABCB1 3435C>T, she thinks the change probably affects the expression level of the protein: the structure is the same, but how much P-gp is made may differ. The slide is more cautious: there is no clear consensus on an effect on P-gp function or expression.",
 quote:"that change in the DNA is affecting something about the protein, it's probably the expression levels of the protein are being affected in some way.",
 note:"Transcript and slide differ in emphasis: she said the change \"affects somehow, maybe the expression levels\"; the slide says \"No clear consensus if there is an effect on P-gp function or expression\". The key uses what both support: it keeps appearing in drug-response studies.",
 cite:"Intro to PGx Lecture Students Fall 2026.pdf slide ‘Synonymous SNP: P-Glycoprotein (P-gp)’ (p. 34); (T) 10/21 transcript"},

{id:"L18-034", lecture:"L18", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L18", sub:"snp", skill:"recall",
 concept:"abcb1-affected-drugs", tags:["smith"], source:"slide",
 stem:"Which drugs are listed as affected by ABCB1 3435C>T?",
 options:[
  {t:"Efavirenz and cyclosporine", correct:true, why:"Both are on the synonymous SNP slide."},
  {t:"Azathioprine and 6-mercaptopurine", correct:false, why:"Those go with TPMT*3A."},
  {t:"Omeprazole and lansoprazole", correct:false, why:"Those go with CYP2C19*3."},
  {t:"Codeine and tamoxifen", correct:false, why:"Those go with CYP2D6*5 and *2xN."}],
 teach:"Each variant slide lists the affected drugs, to show what is coming in the clinical examples: ABCB1 3435C>T, efavirenz and cyclosporine; TPMT*3A, azathioprine and 6-mercaptopurine; CYP2C19*3, proton pump inhibitors (omeprazole, lansoprazole); CYP2D6*5 and *2xN, SSRIs, tamoxifen, codeine and beta blockers.",
 note:"She pointed out that each slide lists affected drugs \"just to give you an idea\" of the coming examples but did not read out the ABCB1 pair.",
 cite:"Intro to PGx Lecture Students Fall 2026.pdf slide ‘Synonymous SNP: P-Glycoprotein (P-gp)’ (p. 34); (T) 10/21 transcript"},

{id:"L18-035", lecture:"L18", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L18", sub:"snp", skill:"term",
 concept:"coding-region", tags:["smith"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"What is the coding region of a gene?",
 options:[
  {t:"The part where the expression machinery starts", correct:false, why:"That is the promoter."},
  {t:"The DNA between genes", correct:false, why:"That is the intergenic region."},
  {t:"The part that becomes RNA and then protein", correct:true, why:"In her words, coding regions are locations that \"directly translate to RNA and then to protein.\""},
  {t:"The stop codon at the end of a gene", correct:false, why:"A stop codon ends translation; it is one codon, not the region."}],
 teach:"Coding regions are locations in a gene that directly translate to RNA and then to protein. Coding SNPs (synonymous, non-synonymous, premature stop) are the ones of interest. On the gene structure figure, exons are the coding parts and introns lie between them.",
 quote:"When I say coding, I'm talking about locations in the genes that directly translate to RNA and then to protein.",
 cite:"Intro to PGx Lecture Students Fall 2026.pdf slide ‘SNPs’ (p. 32); (T) 10/21 transcript"},

/* ---------------- Non-synonymous TPMT*3A and premature stop CYP2C19*3 (pp. 35–36) ---------------- */
{id:"L18-036", lecture:"L18", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L18", sub:"aachange", skill:"apply", core:true, coreWhy:"non-synonymous SNP example",
 concept:"tpmt3a-nonsynonymous", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"TPMT*3A (thiopurine methyltransferase) carries 615 G>A (alanine to threonine) and 874 A>G (tyrosine to cysteine). What kind of single nucleotide polymorphisms (SNPs) are these?",
 options:[
  {t:"Synonymous", correct:false, why:"The amino acids change, so they are not synonymous."},
  {t:"Premature stop codons", correct:false, why:"Neither codon becomes a stop; the protein is made at full length."},
  {t:"Frameshift InDels", correct:false, why:"Each is a single base substitution, not an insertion or deletion."},
  {t:"Non-synonymous", correct:true, why:"Each base change puts a different amino acid into the protein."}],
 teach:"A non-synonymous SNP is a one base pair change that changes the amino acid sequence of the protein. In TPMT*3A, 615 G>A turns GCA (alanine) into ACA (threonine), and 874 A>G turns TAT (tyrosine) into TGT (cysteine). The protein is changed, so these SNPs are non-synonymous.",
 quote:"So the protein has changed, therefore, these two snips are non-synonymous.",
 note:"The transcript renders threonine as \"3anine\".",
 cite:"Intro to PGx Lecture Students Fall 2026.pdf slide ‘Non-Synonymous SNP: TPMT’ (p. 35); (T) 10/21 transcript"},

{id:"L18-037", lecture:"L18", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L18", sub:"aachange", skill:"recall",
 concept:"tpmt-enzyme", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"The TPMT gene codes for which enzyme?",
 options:[
  {t:"Thiopurine methyltransferase", correct:true, why:"TPMT (italic) is the gene; TPMT (not italic) is often used for the enzyme too."},
  {t:"Cytochrome P450 2D6", correct:false, why:"That enzyme is coded by CYP2D6."},
  {t:"N-acetyltransferase 2 (NAT2)", correct:false, why:"That is NAT2, a later example."},
  {t:"Butyrylcholinesterase (BChE)", correct:false, why:"That is BChE, a later example."}],
 teach:"TPMT codes for the enzyme thiopurine methyltransferase. The protein is often called TPMT as well, written without italics. It is an example where the gene symbol and protein name are the same, so italics matter.",
 quote:"We're going to use the gene TPMT. That gene is coding for the, the enzyme thiopurine methyltransferase.",
 cite:"Intro to PGx Lecture Students Fall 2026.pdf slide ‘Non-Synonymous SNP: TPMT’ (p. 35); (T) 10/21 transcript"},

{id:"L18-038", lecture:"L18", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L18", sub:"aachange", skill:"apply", core:true, coreWhy:"lethal consequence she stressed",
 concept:"tpmt3a-consequence", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"What is the functional effect of TPMT*3A, and why does it matter for azathioprine or 6-mercaptopurine?",
 options:[
  {t:"Increased activity; the drug fails", correct:false, why:"TPMT*3A decreases activity."},
  {t:"No change; only a dose note is needed", correct:false, why:"The activity change is significant."},
  {t:"Greatly reduced activity; an untested patient could die", correct:true, why:"These oncology drugs are metabolized by TPMT; without the genotype, a normal dose can kill a patient with this variant."},
  {t:"Slightly reduced activity; only mild side effects follow at normal doses", correct:false, why:"She described a lethal risk, not a mild one."}],
 teach:"TPMT*3A significantly reduces thiopurine methyltransferase activity. Azathioprine and 6-mercaptopurine, which she grouped with oncology medications, are metabolized by TPMT. She warned that a patient with this variant who is not genotyped could be killed.",
 quote:"this is one of those genetic variants that if you don't know the genotype of your patient, you could kill your patient if you didn't genotype them for this snip.",
 cite:"Intro to PGx Lecture Students Fall 2026.pdf slide ‘Non-Synonymous SNP: TPMT’ (p. 35); (T) 10/21 transcript"},

{id:"L18-039", lecture:"L18", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L18", sub:"aachange", skill:"recall", multi:true,
 concept:"tpmt3a-layers", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Which points does the TPMT*3A example illustrate? Select all that apply.",
 options:[
  {t:"A SNP that changes the amino acid sequence", correct:true, why:"Both changes are non-synonymous."},
  {t:"Star names can carry a letter as well as a number", correct:true, why:"*3 became *3A when further work showed it was two SNPs."},
  {t:"One allele can carry more than one change", correct:true, why:"Two loci change together: she called this a haplotype."},
  {t:"A whole gene can be missing", correct:false, why:"That is CYP2D6*5."}],
 teach:"She said the TPMT*3A slide has layers. It shows a non-synonymous SNP; it shows star names with letters (TPMT*3 was renamed *3A when it turned out to be two SNPs); and it shows that one allele can carry changes at two loci, which is a haplotype.",
 quote:"Sometimes when we are using the star nomenclature, instead of just calling the 3. We did some more work and realized that 3 is actually 2 snips in the gene.",
 cite:"Intro to PGx Lecture Students Fall 2026.pdf slide ‘Non-Synonymous SNP: TPMT’ (p. 35); (T) 10/21 transcript"},

{id:"L18-040", lecture:"L18", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L18", sub:"aachange", skill:"apply", core:true, coreWhy:"premature stop codon example; in-class question",
 concept:"cyp2c19-3-stop", tags:["smith","inclass"], source:"both",
 stem:"In CYP2C19*3 (cytochrome P450 2C19), a G>A change turns the codon TGG into TAG. What is the result?",
 options:[
  {t:"Tryptophan is replaced by a different amino acid there", correct:false, why:"TAG codes for no amino acid; it stops translation."},
  {t:"A premature stop codon and no enzyme activity", correct:true, why:"Translation stops early and the protein is too short to have activity."},
  {t:"Extra copies of CYP2C19 are made", correct:false, why:"Extra copies come from copy number variants, as in CYP2D6*2xN."},
  {t:"A frameshift and reduced activity", correct:false, why:"A single substitution does not shift the frame; activity is none, not reduced."}],
 teach:"In wild type CYP2C19 the codon TGG brings in tryptophan, a normal part of the functional protein. In *3 the G becomes A and the codon is TAG, a stop codon: translation stops at that point, well before the normal end. The truncated protein has no activity. She asked whether *3 has low or no activity; the answer was no activity.",
 quote:"Do you guys remember from our previous conversations, what happens to the activity of the enzyme for TC19 when it's the 3 variant? Is it low or no activity? No activity",
 note:"\"TC19\" is the caption's rendering of CYP2C19.",
 cite:"Intro to PGx Lecture Students Fall 2026.pdf slide ‘Premature Stop Codon SNP: CYP2C19’ (p. 36); (T) 10/21 transcript"},

{id:"L18-041", lecture:"L18", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L18", sub:"aachange", skill:"tell",
 concept:"stop-is-nonsynonymous", tags:["smith"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"Is CYP2C19*3 (cytochrome P450 2C19) a synonymous or a non-synonymous single nucleotide polymorphism (SNP)?",
 options:[
  {t:"Synonymous; only a single base pair is changed", correct:false, why:"One changed base pair makes it a SNP; whether it is synonymous depends on the amino acid. Here TGG (tryptophan) becomes TAG, a stop codon, so the sequence changes. She first said synonymous and corrected herself to non-synonymous."},
  {t:"Non-synonymous; it creates a stop codon", correct:true, why:"The amino acid sequence changes: it ends early at the new stop codon."},
  {t:"Neither; it is an InDel", correct:false, why:"It is a single base substitution."},
  {t:"Neither; it is non-coding", correct:false, why:"It is in the coding sequence."}],
 teach:"CYP2C19*3 changes the amino acid sequence, so it is non-synonymous, and specifically it creates a premature stop codon. It is premature because it occurs early in the gene's sequence rather than at the normal end, so the enzyme produced is not functional.",
 quote:"it's a non-synonymous snip, right? It's changing the amino acid sequence, and it's actually creating a stop codon.",
 cite:"Intro to PGx Lecture Students Fall 2026.pdf slide ‘Premature Stop Codon SNP: CYP2C19’ (p. 36); (T) 10/21 transcript"},

{id:"L18-042", lecture:"L18", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L18", sub:"aachange", skill:"recall",
 concept:"cyp2c19-3-drugs", tags:["smith"], source:"slide",
 stem:"Which drug class is listed as affected by CYP2C19*3 (cytochrome P450 2C19)?",
 options:[
  {t:"Thiopurines", correct:false, why:"Azathioprine and 6-mercaptopurine go with TPMT*3A."},
  {t:"Proton pump inhibitors", correct:true, why:"Omeprazole and lansoprazole are listed."},
  {t:"Selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors", correct:false, why:"SSRIs go with CYP2D6*5 and *2xN."},
  {t:"Beta blockers", correct:false, why:"Beta blockers go with CYP2D6*5 and *2xN."}],
 teach:"The premature stop codon slide lists proton pump inhibitors (omeprazole, lansoprazole) as affected by CYP2C19*3. She noted that the affected drugs on these slides preview the clinical examples to come.",
 note:"Slide only; she referred to the affected-drug lists on these slides in general.",
 cite:"Intro to PGx Lecture Students Fall 2026.pdf slide ‘Premature Stop Codon SNP: CYP2C19’ (p. 36); (T) 10/21 transcript"},

{id:"L18-043", lecture:"L18", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L18", sub:"aachange", skill:"term",
 concept:"synonymous-table-answer", tags:["smith"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"Which general definition of a synonymous SNP did she give as a model answer for the variant table?",
 options:[
  {t:"A base pair changes but the amino acid sequence stays the same", correct:true, why:"Her model answer, in general terms without a specific gene."},
  {t:"A base pair changes and a different amino acid is put into the protein", correct:false, why:"That is non-synonymous."},
  {t:"A base pair changes and translation stops early", correct:false, why:"That is a premature stop codon."},
  {t:"A few base pairs are added and the frame shifts", correct:false, why:"That is an InDel causing a frameshift."}],
 teach:"For the types-of-variants think/pair/share table she asked for general terms rather than specific examples: what the variant is at the molecular level and what might happen to the gene's product. Her model answer for a synonymous SNP: a base pair in the DNA gets changed, but the amino acid sequence in the protein stays the same as the wild type.",
 quote:"A synonymous snip is a change to the DNA sequence, a base pair gets changed. But the amino acid sequence in the protein stays the same compared to the wild type.",
 cite:"Intro to PGx Lecture Students Fall 2026.pdf slide ‘Think/Pair/Share exercise for Types of Genetic Variants’ (p. 41); (T) 10/21 transcript"},

/* ---------------- InDels and tandem repeats (pp. 37–38) ---------------- */
{id:"L18-044", lecture:"L18", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L18", sub:"indel", skill:"apply", core:true, coreWhy:"InDel and frameshift mechanism",
 concept:"indel-frameshift", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"An insertion/deletion (InDel): a G is inserted so that ATGC becomes ATGGC in a coding region. What usually happens to the protein from that point on?",
 options:[
  {t:"Only the one amino acid at the insertion site changes", correct:false, why:"Every codon after the insertion is read out of frame, not just one."},
  {t:"Nothing changes", correct:false, why:"The reading frame shifts, so the amino acids change."},
  {t:"The amino acid sequence changes from there on", correct:true, why:"The frameshift alters all later codons; a premature stop codon may also be created."},
  {t:"The gene is copied", correct:false, why:"Copying a whole gene is a copy number variant."}],
 teach:"An InDel is the insertion or deletion of a few nucleotides (her examples: one, or usually 2, 3 or maybe 5). It shifts the three-letter code, so from the point of the InDel on the codons are out of register and the protein can be completely different; sometimes a premature stop codon is created. Either way, the activity of the gene's product can change.",
 quote:"When you do these indels, you end up causing a frame shift. That means you're shifting the three-letter code coding for amino acids",
 cite:"Intro to PGx Lecture Students Fall 2026.pdf slide ‘Small Insertion/Deletion (InDel)’ (p. 37); (T) 10/21 transcript"},

{id:"L18-045", lecture:"L18", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L18", sub:"indel", skill:"recall", multi:true,
 concept:"indel-outcomes", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Which outcomes can an insertion/deletion (InDel) in a coding region produce? Select all that apply.",
 options:[
  {t:"Incorrect amino acids after the InDel", correct:true, why:"The shifted three-letter code brings in the wrong amino acids."},
  {t:"A premature stop codon", correct:true, why:"A stop codon may be generated, ending the protein early."},
  {t:"Changed activity of the gene's product", correct:true, why:"InDels can definitely change the activity."},
  {t:"Extra copies of the whole gene", correct:false, why:"That is a copy number variant, not an InDel."}],
 teach:"InDels shift the three-letter code, putting incorrect amino acids into the protein, and may generate a premature stop codon. Because the sequence after the InDel can be entirely different, the activity of the gene's product can change. The slide adds that inserting or deleting exactly three nucleotides adds or removes one amino acid.",
 quote:"So these indels can definitely change the activity.",
 cite:"Intro to PGx Lecture Students Fall 2026.pdf slide ‘Small Insertion/Deletion (InDel)’ (p. 37); (T) 10/21 transcript"},

{id:"L18-046", lecture:"L18", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L18", sub:"indel", skill:"tell", lowYield:true,
 concept:"indel-three-vs-one", tags:["smith"], source:"slide",
 stem:"How does a three-nucleotide insertion/deletion (InDel) differ from a one-nucleotide InDel in a coding region?",
 options:[
  {t:"Three adds or removes one amino acid; one shifts the frame", correct:true, why:"A multiple of three keeps the frame; a frameshift needs a number that is not a multiple of 3."},
  {t:"Three shifts the frame; one adds or removes one amino acid", correct:false, why:"Reversed."},
  {t:"Both shift the frame equally", correct:false, why:"Three nucleotides is a whole codon, so the frame is kept."},
  {t:"Neither changes the protein", correct:false, why:"Both change it: one by an amino acid, one by a frameshift."}],
 teach:"A frameshift happens when the number of nucleotides added or lost is not a multiple of 3, which disrupts the reading frame. Deleting or inserting exactly three nucleotides removes or adds one amino acid, which can still affect enzyme activity. Her spoken example inserted one G (ATGC to ATGGC).",
 note:"Slide wording (pp. 31 and 37), not spoken. She described InDels of 'usually 2 or 3, maybe 5' nucleotides as causing a frame shift without separating the three-nucleotide case, so treat this as a slide detail.",
 cite:"Intro to PGx Lecture Students Fall 2026.pdf slides ‘List of genetic variant types’ (p. 31), ‘Small Insertion/Deletion (InDel)’ (p. 37); (T) 10/21 transcript"},

{id:"L18-047", lecture:"L18", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L18", sub:"indel", skill:"tell",
 concept:"micro-vs-minisatellite", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"How are microsatellites and minisatellites distinguished?",
 options:[
  {t:"Micro: in genes; mini: between genes", correct:false, why:"Both can be found in intergenic and in coding regions."},
  {t:"Micro: fewer than 5 base pairs; mini: more than 5", correct:true, why:"The length of the repeated unit separates them."},
  {t:"Micro: one copy; mini: many copies", correct:false, why:"Both are repeated, and the number of repeats varies between people."},
  {t:"Micro: more than 5 base pairs; mini: fewer than 5", correct:false, why:"Reversed."}],
 teach:"Variable number tandem repeats (VNTRs) are short DNA sequences inserted again and again in a row, and the number of repeats differs between people. Microsatellites have a unit of fewer than 5 base pairs (also called short tandem repeats or simple sequence repeats); minisatellites have more than 5. Because tandem repeats are quite unique to each person, they are used in forensic and paternity testing.",
 quote:"the insertions could be either microsatellites, which are less than 5 base pairs, or mini satellites are those that are greater than 5 base pairs",
 cite:"Intro to PGx Lecture Students Fall 2026.pdf slide ‘Tandem Repeats’ (p. 38); (T) 10/21 transcript"},

{id:"L18-048", lecture:"L18", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L18", sub:"indel", skill:"apply",
 concept:"tandem-repeat-location", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Where does a tandem repeat most clearly cause problems with the gene's product?",
 options:[
  {t:"In an intergenic region", correct:false, why:"Intergenic repeats often show no change in gene function; their effect on expression is still being studied."},
  {t:"In the coding sequence of the gene", correct:true, why:"In the coding sequence a repeat acts like an InDel and can throw off the amino acid sequence."},
  {t:"Only on the stop codon", correct:false, why:"She gave no such rule."},
  {t:"Anywhere in the genome, with equal effect", correct:false, why:"Location matters: coding repeats change the protein, intergenic ones often do not."}],
 teach:"Many VNTRs lie in intergenic regions (once called junk DNA) with no apparent function; researchers are looking at whether these affect expression. A tandem repeat within the coding sequence acts like an InDel: it can throw off the amino acid sequence and produce abnormal gene products.",
 quote:"if you get these tandem repeats within the coding sequence of the gene, it's like an indel.",
 cite:"Intro to PGx Lecture Students Fall 2026.pdf slide ‘Tandem Repeats’ (p. 38); (T) 10/21 transcript"},

/* ---------------- Gene deletion and copy number (pp. 39–40) ---------------- */
{id:"L18-049", lecture:"L18", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L18", sub:"cnv", skill:"recall", core:true, coreWhy:"CYP2D6*5 example",
 concept:"cyp2d6-5-deletion", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"What is CYP2D6*5 (cytochrome P450 2D6)?",
 options:[
  {t:"A premature stop codon in CYP2D6", correct:false, why:"The premature stop example is CYP2C19*3."},
  {t:"Extra copies of the CYP2D6 gene on one chromosome", correct:false, why:"That is CYP2D6*2xN."},
  {t:"The whole CYP2D6 gene deleted from one allele", correct:true, why:"Thousands of base pairs that make up the gene are gone."},
  {t:"A synonymous SNP in CYP2D6", correct:false, why:"A SNP changes one base; *5 removes the gene."}],
 teach:"In CYP2D6*5 the entire gene for cytochrome P450 2D6 is missing from that allele: not a single nucleotide change but thousands of base pairs deleted. The functional effect is loss of function (null activity) from that allele. Her Quiz 4 asked for the name of this variant.",
 quote:"Total gene is gone",
 cite:"Intro to PGx Lecture Students Fall 2026.pdf slide ‘Gene Deletion CYP2D6’ (p. 39); (T) 10/21 transcript; Smith_Quiz_4_-_Intro_to_Pharmacogenomics.pdf Q1"},

{id:"L18-050", lecture:"L18", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L18", sub:"cnv", skill:"apply",
 concept:"cyp2d6-5-zygosity", tags:["smith"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"How much cytochrome P450 2D6 (CYP2D6) activity does a CYP2D6*1/*5 person have, compared with a CYP2D6*5/*5 person?",
 options:[
  {t:"*1/*5: none; *5/*5: none", correct:false, why:"The chromosome with the gene still makes CYP2D6."},
  {t:"*1/*5: normal; *5/*5: reduced", correct:false, why:"*5/*5 has no gene to make any enzyme."},
  {t:"*1/*5: some; *5/*5: none", correct:true, why:"One working copy gives some activity; with both copies deleted there is none."},
  {t:"*1/*5: increased; *5/*5: some", correct:false, why:"A deletion never adds activity."}],
 teach:"A person with *5 on one allele and wild type on the other has some CYP2D6 activity, because the chromosome that has the gene still produces the enzyme. A person homozygous *5/*5 has no CYP2D6 activity. People with too little CYP2D6 activity are called poor metabolizers.",
 quote:"If you have a person that's homozygous, 2D65, so they're 55, they don't have any 2D6 activity",
 note:"\"2D65\" and \"55\" are the caption's renderings of CYP2D6*5 and *5/*5.",
 cite:"Intro to PGx Lecture Students Fall 2026.pdf slide ‘Gene Deletion CYP2D6’ (p. 39); (T) 10/21 transcript"},

{id:"L18-051", lecture:"L18", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L18", sub:"cnv", skill:"tell", core:true, coreWhy:"PM vs UM phenotype names",
 concept:"cyp2d6-5-vs-2xn", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Which phenotype names go with CYP2D6*5 (cytochrome P450 2D6) and CYP2D6*2xN?",
 options:[
  {t:"*5: ultrarapid metabolizer; *2xN: poor metabolizer", correct:false, why:"Reversed."},
  {t:"*5: poor metabolizer; *2xN: ultrarapid metabolizer", correct:true, why:"No gene means little or no enzyme; extra gene copies mean a lot of enzyme."},
  {t:"Both: poor metabolizer", correct:false, why:"Extra copies raise activity."},
  {t:"Both: intermediate metabolizer", correct:false, why:"Neither is the middle group."}],
 teach:"CYP2D6*5 removes the gene, giving loss of function and a poor metabolizer (PM) phenotype. CYP2D6*2xN carries extra copies of the gene, so these people make a lot of CYP2D6 and are called ultrarapid metabolizers (UM). The same drugs are affected in both cases.",
 quote:"we just started calling those people ultra rapid metabolizers. That's what we call their phenotype, ultra rapid metabolizer.",
 cite:"Intro to PGx Lecture Students Fall 2026.pdf slides ‘Gene Deletion CYP2D6’ (p. 39), ‘Copy Number Variant CYP2D6’ (p. 40); (T) 10/21 transcript"},

{id:"L18-052", lecture:"L18", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L18", sub:"cnv", skill:"apply",
 concept:"cyp2d6-2x2", tags:["smith","inclass"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"A person carries two copies of the cytochrome P450 2D6 (CYP2D6) gene on one chromosome. How is this written more precisely than CYP2D6*2xN?",
 options:[
  {t:"CYP2D6*2/*2", correct:false, why:"That is a genotype with *2 on each chromosome, not two copies on one."},
  {t:"CYP2D6*5", correct:false, why:"*5 is the deletion."},
  {t:"CYP2D6*2x2", correct:true, why:"N is replaced by the number of copies."},
  {t:"CYP2D6*2N2", correct:false, why:"The x stays; only the N is replaced by the number."}],
 teach:"In the copy number variant CYP2D6*2xN, N stands for the number of copies. She asked how many copies the drawn person had (two) and wrote it as CYP2D6*2x2. She said people have been found with 3, 4 or 5 copies; the slide lists N = 2, 3, 4, 5 and 13.",
 quote:"So we would really call this person a 2D6 2 x 2, because they've got two copies and define their genotype a little bit more.",
 note:"Spoken \"345 copies\" read as 3, 4, 5 copies, matching the slide's N = 2, 3, 4, 5, 13.",
 cite:"Intro to PGx Lecture Students Fall 2026.pdf slide ‘Copy Number Variant CYP2D6’ (p. 40); (T) 10/21 transcript"},

{id:"L18-053", lecture:"L18", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L18", sub:"cnv", skill:"recall", multi:true,
 concept:"cyp2d6-affected-drugs", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Which drugs or drug classes are affected by CYP2D6*5 (cytochrome P450 2D6) and CYP2D6*2xN? Select all that apply.",
 options:[
  {t:"SSRIs", correct:true, why:"Listed on both CYP2D6 slides and said aloud."},
  {t:"Tamoxifen", correct:true, why:"Listed on both CYP2D6 slides and said aloud."},
  {t:"Codeine", correct:true, why:"Listed on both CYP2D6 slides and said aloud."},
  {t:"Beta blockers", correct:true, why:"Listed on both CYP2D6 slides and said aloud."},
  {t:"Azathioprine", correct:false, why:"Azathioprine is affected by TPMT variants."}],
 teach:"Both CYP2D6 variants, the deletion (*5) and the extra copies (*2xN), affect the same drugs: SSRIs, tamoxifen, codeine and beta blockers. One gives too little CYP2D6 activity and the other too much.",
 quote:"Some drugs that we'll talk about, SSRIs and tamoxifen and codeine and beta blockers, they're all affected by the presence of a 2D6 5.",
 cite:"Intro to PGx Lecture Students Fall 2026.pdf slides ‘Gene Deletion CYP2D6’ (p. 39), ‘Copy Number Variant CYP2D6’ (p. 40); (T) 10/21 transcript"},

/* ---------------- Genotype-guided dosing (Examples pp. 5–7) ---------------- */
{id:"L18-054", lecture:"L18", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L18", sub:"dosing", skill:"figure",
 concept:"warfarin-clearance-genotype", tags:["smith"], source:"slide",
 stem:"A bar graph (Scordo et al. 2002) plots S-warfarin unbound clearance (mL/min, axis 0 to 1000) on the y-axis against six cytochrome P450 2C9 (CYP2C9) genotypes on the x-axis: *1/*1, *1/*2, *1/*3, *2/*2, *2/*3 and *3/*3. Which genotype has the lowest clearance?",
 options:[
  {t:"CYP2C9*1/*1", correct:false, why:"Wild type has the highest clearance."},
  {t:"CYP2C9*1/*3", correct:false, why:"One wild type allele keeps clearance in the middle."},
  {t:"CYP2C9*2/*2", correct:false, why:"Low, but above *3/*3."},
  {t:"CYP2C9*3/*3", correct:true, why:"*3/*3 has the lowest unbound clearance of the six genotypes, under 100 mL/min."}],
 teach:"The slide shows that the rate of warfarin metabolism (a phenotype) depends on CYP2C9 genotype. Unbound S-warfarin clearance is highest in *1/*1, lower with one variant allele (*1/*2, *1/*3), lower still in *2/*2 and *2/*3, and lowest in *3/*3. Carrying at least one variant allele decreases clearance.",
 note:"This slide fell in the part of 10/21 missing from the lecture capture (she offered to record and post it); on 10/22 she referred back to it (\"we looked at that already in a graph and we looked at warfarin, uh, clearance\").",
 cite:"PGx-Examples-Students-Fall-2026.pdf slide ‘Genotype/Phenotype Relationship’ (p. 5); not captured in the 10/21 transcript"},

{id:"L18-055", lecture:"L18", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L18", sub:"dosing", skill:"figure", core:true, coreWhy:"a figure she said she really likes; patient A's dose",
 concept:"patient-a-low-dose", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"In a figure comparing four patients, A to D, the allele status of a metabolism gene is drawn under each: A has both copies crossed out, B one copy crossed out, C two intact copies and D three intact copies. A bar chart of percentage of average dose (axis 0 to 300) gives each patient a gray bar at 100% and a white bar for a dose based on genotype, and above each patient two concentration-versus-time curves show the resulting levels, one solid and one dashed with the area under it shaded gray. Patient A has two non-functional alleles of the enzyme that clears the drug. Given the standard dose, what happens, and how is the dose adjusted?",
 options:[
  {t:"Concentration too high; lower the dose", correct:true, why:"Patient A cannot clear the drug through that enzyme; a lower, genotype-based dose keeps the level at efficacy but out of toxicity."},
  {t:"Concentration too low; raise the dose", correct:false, why:"That is patient D, with extra gene copies."},
  {t:"Concentration normal; no change", correct:false, why:"That is the wild type patient."},
  {t:"Concentration too high; stop the drug entirely", correct:false, why:"In the patients A to D comparison patient A's dose is lowered, not stopped."}],
 teach:"In the figure the gray bars are everyone at 100% of the average dose, and the curves show the resulting concentrations. Patient A, with no working copy of the metabolizing enzyme, cannot metabolize and clear the drug, so the standard dose gives too high a level. Dosing by genotype gives patient A a lower dose and brings the level to efficacy without toxicity.",
 quote:"We know they don't have any 2D6, so they can't metabolize it and clear it. So we're going to lower their dose, give them a dose based on their genotype, which is lower, because they can't metabolize it.",
 note:"The figure (Foye's) labels only a \"metabolism gene\"; she used CYP2D6 as the example. The first part of her explanation of this figure was missing from the lecture capture.",
 cite:"PGx-Examples-Students-Fall-2026.pdf slide ‘Genotype/Phenotype Relationship 2’ (p. 6); (T) 10/21 transcript"},

{id:"L18-056", lecture:"L18", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L18", sub:"dosing", skill:"figure",
 concept:"patient-d-high-dose", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"In a figure comparing four patients, A to D, the allele status of a metabolism gene is drawn under each: A has both copies crossed out, B one copy crossed out, C two intact copies and D three intact copies. A bar chart of percentage of average dose (axis 0 to 300) gives each patient a gray bar at 100% and a white bar for a dose based on genotype, and above each patient two concentration-versus-time curves show the resulting levels, one solid and one dashed with the area under it shaded gray. Patient D has multiple copies of the metabolizing enzyme gene. What happens on the standard dose?",
 options:[
  {t:"Area under the curve (AUC) is too high and toxic", correct:false, why:"That is patient A."},
  {t:"AUC is too low and never therapeutic", correct:true, why:"The drug is metabolized so quickly that the level does not stay therapeutic; a higher dose fixes it."},
  {t:"AUC is normal", correct:false, why:"Extra copies clear the drug faster than average."},
  {t:"The drug cannot be absorbed", correct:false, why:"The gene is a metabolizing enzyme, not an absorption step."}],
 teach:"Patient D has so many copies of the metabolizing enzyme that the regular dose is metabolized away quickly; the area under the curve (AUC) is very low and the patient never stays therapeutic. Giving patient D a higher dose based on genotype brings the AUC up to the same therapeutic range as everyone else.",
 quote:"If you just gave them the regular old dose, they've got so many copies of the enzyme that if you give them the regular old dose, not based on their genotype. They just metabolize it so quickly.",
 cite:"PGx-Examples-Students-Fall-2026.pdf slide ‘Genotype/Phenotype Relationship 2’ (p. 6); (T) 10/21 transcript"},

{id:"L18-057", lecture:"L18", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L18", sub:"dosing", skill:"figure",
 concept:"same-auc-why", tags:["smith","inclass"], source:"both",
 stem:"In a figure comparing four patients, A to D, the allele status of a metabolism gene is drawn under each: A has both copies crossed out, B one copy crossed out, C two intact copies and D three intact copies. A bar chart of percentage of average dose (axis 0 to 300) gives each patient a gray bar at 100% and a white bar for a dose based on genotype, and above each patient two concentration-versus-time curves show the resulting levels, one solid and one dashed with the area under it shaded gray. With genotype-based dosing, all four patients reach the same therapeutic area under the curve (AUC). Why are their doses different?",
 options:[
  {t:"They differ in how fast their enzymes clear the drug", correct:true, why:"Each dose is matched to how fast that patient's enzymes clear the drug."},
  {t:"They differ in body weight", correct:false, why:"The patients A to D comparison varies only the allele status of the metabolism gene."},
  {t:"Each patient has a different drug target for this drug", correct:false, why:"The gene in the patients A to D comparison is a metabolizing enzyme (PK), not a target."},
  {t:"Their kidneys differ", correct:false, why:"Renal function is not the variable shown."}],
 teach:"She asked why everyone ends up with the same gray AUC. Because of their genotypes, the four groups have different metabolizing capability, so each needs a different dose for the same exposure. Dosing by genotype gets everyone therapeutic and nobody toxic.",
 quote:"Yeah, because of their genotypes, these 4 groups of people have different metabolizing capabilities.",
 note:"She said she wished the publisher had switched the colors of the AUC shading: in the figure the white bars are the genotype-based doses and the gray shading is the resulting AUC.",
 cite:"PGx-Examples-Students-Fall-2026.pdf slide ‘Genotype/Phenotype Relationship 2’ (p. 6); (T) 10/21 transcript"},

{id:"L18-058", lecture:"L18", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L18", sub:"dosing", skill:"tell", core:true, coreWhy:"goal of pharmacogenomics; efficacy vs toxicity",
 concept:"efficacy-vs-toxicity-ad", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Patient A has both copies of a metabolism gene non-functional and receives a lower dose; patient D has three functional copies and receives a higher dose. By dosing A and D on their genotypes, which clinical outcome was achieved for each?",
 options:[
  {t:"A: efficacy ensured; D: toxicity prevented", correct:false, why:"Reversed."},
  {t:"A: toxicity prevented; D: efficacy ensured", correct:true, why:"A's low dose kept the AUC out of the toxic zone; D's high dose kept the drug from being cleared before it worked."},
  {t:"Both: toxicity prevented", correct:false, why:"D was at risk of no effect, not toxicity."},
  {t:"Both: efficacy ensured", correct:false, why:"A was at risk of toxicity."}],
 teach:"Genes can drive kinetics or dynamics, but either way the clinical outcome is efficacy or toxicity, and the goal of pharmacogenomics is to ensure efficacy and prevent toxicity. For patient A the low dose prevented toxicity. For patient D the high dose ensured efficacy: the drug was not metabolized away before reaching an effective level.",
 quote:"the goal of pharmacogenomics is to ensure efficacy and prevent toxicity.",
 cite:"PGx-Examples-Students-Fall-2026.pdf slides ‘Genotype/Phenotype Relationship 2’ (p. 6), ‘Impact of Pharmacogenomics on PK and PD’ (p. 7); (T) 10/21 transcript"},

{id:"L18-059", lecture:"L18", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L18", sub:"dosing", skill:"figure",
 concept:"gray-bars-100", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"In a figure comparing four patients, A to D, the allele status of a metabolism gene is drawn under each: A has both copies crossed out, B one copy crossed out, C two intact copies and D three intact copies. A bar chart of percentage of average dose (axis 0 to 300) gives each patient a gray bar at 100% and a white bar for a dose based on genotype, and above each patient two concentration-versus-time curves show the resulting levels, one solid and one dashed with the area under it shaded gray. What do the gray dose bars at 100% represent?",
 options:[
  {t:"Each patient's own genotype-based dose of the drug", correct:false, why:"Those are the white bars."},
  {t:"The same average dose given to everyone", correct:true, why:"Everyone getting a gray dose gets the same dose, 100% of average."},
  {t:"The toxic threshold", correct:false, why:"The bars are doses, not a threshold."},
  {t:"Enzyme activity", correct:false, why:"The y-axis is percentage of average dose."}],
 teach:"The bars at the bottom of the figure are percentage of the average dose. Gray bars: every patient at 100%, the same dose, producing very different concentration curves. White bars: doses adjusted to genotype, lower for patient A and much higher for patient D, giving everyone the same therapeutic AUC.",
 quote:"The gray dose bars at the bottom, remember this is saying they are at 100% of the average dose here. Everybody getting a gray dose, they're getting the same dose.",
 cite:"PGx-Examples-Students-Fall-2026.pdf slide ‘Genotype/Phenotype Relationship 2’ (p. 6); (T) 10/21 transcript"},

/* ---------------- Gene structure and population frequencies (Examples pp. 8–9) ---------------- */
{id:"L18-060", lecture:"L18", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L18", sub:"popfreq", skill:"recall",
 concept:"gene-structure", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"A Goodman and Gilman's diagram of gene structure labels five parts along a gene: two exons, an intron, an intergenic region and a promoter. Boxes above and below mark variants changing protein abundance, variants changing the protein product, and variants serving as markers for nearby or distant functional variation. Which region is where the machinery for expressing the gene starts?",
 options:[
  {t:"Exon", correct:false, why:"Exons are the parts that end up as amino acid sequence."},
  {t:"Intron", correct:false, why:"Introns lie between exons; variations can occur there too."},
  {t:"Intergenic region", correct:false, why:"That is DNA between genes."},
  {t:"Promoter", correct:true, why:"The promoter is the part of the gene where the expression machinery starts."}],
 teach:"A gene has a promoter, where the machinery for expressing the gene starts; exons, the DNA that eventually becomes amino acid sequence; introns between them, once thought meaningless, where variations are now known to occur; and the stop codon. The figure groups variants into those changing protein abundance, those changing the protein product, and markers for nearby functional variation.",
 quote:"The intronic regions, we used to think they didn't mean anything, but they do now. We know that that variations can occur in intronic regions.",
 cite:"PGx-Examples-Students-Fall-2026.pdf slide ‘Basic changes to genes’ (p. 8); (T) 10/21 transcript"},

{id:"L18-061", lecture:"L18", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L18", sub:"popfreq", skill:"figure",
 concept:"cyp3a5-europeans", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"In the cytochrome P450 3A5 (CYP3A5) allele frequency panel, what is true of the European population?",
 options:[
  {t:"Most carry *1, the wild type", correct:false, why:"Relatively few Europeans are wild type."},
  {t:"Most carry *3, a non-functional allele", correct:true, why:"Most of the European bar is *3 (no function)."},
  {t:"Most carry *6 or *7", correct:false, why:"*6 and *7 appear in the sub-Saharan African bar."},
  {t:"Allele frequencies match East Asians exactly", correct:false, why:"The frequencies differ across populations."}],
 teach:"Panel A shows CYP3A5 allele frequencies for sub-Saharan African, European and East Asian populations: *1 (wild type) and the non-functional *3, *6 and *7. In the sub-Saharan African population *1 is common and *3, *6 and *7 all appear; among Europeans relatively few are wild type and most carry *3. The point is that frequencies are very different across populations.",
 quote:"The Europeans that were in the study, where relatively few of them are wild type",
 cite:"PGx-Examples-Students-Fall-2026.pdf slide ‘Allele and Phenotype Frequencies’ (p. 9); (T) 10/21 transcript"},

{id:"L18-062", lecture:"L18", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L18", sub:"popfreq", skill:"apply", core:true, coreWhy:"the number she stressed: less than 10% of the normal dose",
 concept:"tpmt-pm-dose", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"A thiopurine methyltransferase (TPMT) poor metabolizer needs azathioprine or 6-mercaptopurine. What dose does this patient need?",
 options:[
  {t:"Less than 10% of the normal dose", correct:true, why:"A normal dose could kill a TPMT poor metabolizer."},
  {t:"About half the normal dose", correct:false, why:"Far too much for a poor metabolizer."},
  {t:"The normal dose", correct:false, why:"That is for normal metabolizers."},
  {t:"Twice the normal dose, to overcome slow clearance", correct:false, why:"A poor metabolizer cannot clear the drug, so more would be dangerous."}],
 teach:"Azathioprine and 6-mercaptopurine are metabolized by TPMT. In the TPMT phenotype panel, most people are normal metabolizers, and only a small bar are poor metabolizers. A poor metabolizer given the normal dose could die; these patients need less than 10% of the normal dose.",
 quote:"You could kill them. Those people need less than 10% of the normal dose.",
 cite:"PGx-Examples-Students-Fall-2026.pdf slide ‘Allele and Phenotype Frequencies’ (p. 9); (T) 10/21 transcript"},

{id:"L18-063", lecture:"L18", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L18", sub:"popfreq", skill:"apply",
 concept:"why-frequency-studies", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Why do researchers measure how often variant alleles occur in different populations?",
 options:[
  {t:"Frequencies are the same everywhere, so one study suffices", correct:false, why:"Frequencies are really different across populations."},
  {t:"To judge how often to worry about, or test for, a variant", correct:true, why:"Even a rare variant matters if the toxicity is severe, as with TPMT poor metabolizers."},
  {t:"To name new star alleles", correct:false, why:"Naming comes from characterizing the variant, not from its frequency."},
  {t:"To replace genotyping of individual patients", correct:false, why:"Frequencies guide decisions about genotyping; they do not replace it."}],
 teach:"A lot of work goes into measuring how often each version of a gene occurs in different populations, and the frequencies differ widely. Knowing the frequency, together with how severe the toxicity is, helps decide how often to worry about a variant and whether everyone from a population should be genotyped. She said the graphs themselves need not be memorized.",
 quote:"how often should we, how often do we need to worry about this in a population, right? OK, how do we need to genotype everybody from that population? Is it such a bad side effect or a toxicity?",
 note:"She first joked that the graphs must be memorized, then: \"OK, so you don't have to memorize this.\" The Examples objectives slide (p. 3) lists \"Know the expected frequency of the most clinically significant of these enzyme variants\"; on 10/22 she repeated that the frequencies need not be memorized, only how they vary.",
 cite:"PGx-Examples-Students-Fall-2026.pdf slide ‘Allele and Phenotype Frequencies’ (p. 9); (T) 10/21 transcript"},

{id:"L18-064", lecture:"L18", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L18", sub:"popfreq", skill:"figure", multi:true,
 concept:"freq-figure-genes", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"A four-panel Goodman and Gilman's figure gives allele and phenotype frequencies in sub-Saharan African, European and East Asian populations. Panel A shows allele frequencies of *1 (wild type) and the no-function *3, *6 and *7. Panel B shows allele frequencies of the -1639G>A variant (G, higher dose required; A, lower dose required). Panel C shows metabolizer phenotypes (indeterminate, normal, intermediate, poor) for a thiopurine-metabolizing enzyme. Panel D shows the same phenotypes for a gene she said will be discussed later, with sub-Saharan African frequencies marked currently unknown. Which genes are shown in this figure? Select all that apply.",
 options:[
  {t:"CYP3A5", correct:true, why:"Panel A, allele frequencies."},
  {t:"VKORC1", correct:true, why:"Panel B, -1639G>A allele frequencies."},
  {t:"TPMT", correct:true, why:"Panel C, phenotypes."},
  {t:"NUDT15", correct:true, why:"Panel D, phenotypes; she said it will be discussed later."},
  {t:"CYP2D6", correct:false, why:"CYP2D6 frequencies come on the CYP2D6 example slides, not in this figure."}],
 teach:"The Goodman and Gilman's figure shows four genes across populations: CYP3A5 allele frequencies, VKORC1 -1639G>A allele frequencies (G, higher dose required; A, lower dose required), TPMT phenotypes and NUDT15 phenotypes. Each is discussed with its drug later in the examples.",
 quote:"So this figure just gives you examples of 4 different genes.",
 cite:"PGx-Examples-Students-Fall-2026.pdf slide ‘Allele and Phenotype Frequencies’ (p. 9); (T) 10/21 transcript"},

{id:"L18-065", lecture:"L18", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L18", sub:"aachange", skill:"tell", multi:true,
 concept:"no-activity-alleles", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Which alleles produce no active enzyme from that copy of the gene? Select all that apply.",
 options:[
  {t:"CYP2C19*2", correct:true, why:"A truncated, non-functional protein."},
  {t:"CYP2C9*2", correct:false, why:"Decreased activity, not none."},
  {t:"CYP2C19*3", correct:true, why:"A premature stop codon (TGG to TAG) truncates the protein."},
  {t:"CYP2D6*5", correct:true, why:"The whole gene is deleted, so nothing is made from that allele."},
  {t:"CYP2D6*2xN", correct:false, why:"Extra copies: more activity, an ultrarapid metabolizer."}],
 teach:"Three different mechanisms give an allele with no activity: CYP2C19*2 makes a short non-functional protein, CYP2C19*3 has a premature stop codon, and CYP2D6*5 has no gene at all. CYP2C9*2 and *3 only decrease activity, and CYP2D6*2xN increases it. The same star number means different things in different genes.",
 quote:"So, it's a whole gene is gone. They just don't have it.",
 cite:"Intro to PGx Lecture Students Fall 2026.pdf slides ‘More Allele “Star” Nomenclature’ (p. 26), ‘Premature Stop Codon SNP: CYP2C19’ (p. 36), ‘Gene Deletion CYP2D6’ (p. 39), ‘Copy Number Variant CYP2D6’ (p. 40); (T) 10/21 transcript"},

{id:"L18-066", lecture:"L18", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L18", sub:"dosing", skill:"figure", multi:true,
 concept:"genotype-dosing-results", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"In a figure comparing four patients, A to D, the allele status of a metabolism gene is drawn under each: A has both copies crossed out, B one copy crossed out, C two intact copies and D three intact copies. A bar chart of percentage of average dose (axis 0 to 300) gives each patient a gray bar at 100% and a white bar for a dose based on genotype, and above each patient two concentration-versus-time curves show the resulting levels, one solid and one dashed with the area under it shaded gray. Which statements describe dosing by genotype (the white bars) and its result? Select all that apply.",
 options:[
  {t:"Patient A gets less than the average dose", correct:true, why:"Patient A cannot clear the drug through the enzyme."},
  {t:"Patient D gets much more than the average dose", correct:true, why:"Patient D's extra copies clear the drug quickly."},
  {t:"Every patient ends with the same therapeutic AUC", correct:true, why:"Matching dose to metabolizing capability gives everyone the same exposure."},
  {t:"Every patient gets 100% of the average dose", correct:false, why:"That is the gray bars: the same dose for everyone, which gives different exposures."}],
 teach:"The figure compares two ways of dosing four patients whose metabolizing gene status differs. With the same dose (gray bars, 100% of average) their concentrations differ widely. With genotype-based doses (white bars) patient A gets less and patient D much more, and every patient ends up with the same therapeutic area under the curve (AUC), so everyone is therapeutic and nobody toxic.",
 quote:"So by dosing people based on their genotype, Which would be the white dosing. You adjust the amount of drug they get, so that their metabolic capabilities result in everybody getting the gray AUC and everybody gets therapeutic",
 cite:"PGx-Examples-Students-Fall-2026.pdf slide ‘Genotype/Phenotype Relationship 2’ (p. 6); (T) 10/21 transcript"}
);
